crisis in Greece the country remains  divided over the terms of a new bailout  package which the Greek parliament  approved last Thursday the deal includes  a painful mix of tax hikes spending  curbs and pension cuts as well as  opening shops on Sundays we spoke to the  journalist Philip pangalos in Athens yes  it's a big day today Greek banks will  finally reopen after three weeks since  capital controls were imposed it's going  to be interesting I expect to have some  cues in the banks today the reality is  that Greeks will still only be able to  withdraw 60 euros a day from their  accounts even though there is a  provision to let them withdraw a  cumulative 420 euros a week saving them  having to either go to the bank or to  ATMs each day so that'll make life a  little bit easier there are a few other  measures that are being introduced to  help restore normality people be able to  deposit checks not cash them in a bid to  allow businesses that have got checks  that a post-dated get them into their  bank accounts and there's also going to  be an allowance for Greek families that  have their children studying abroad to  send 5,000 euros a term abroad so that  will help them as well another  interesting point is is it'll be the  first time that Greeks can actually  access their safety deposit boxes since  the banks are closed and the key point  here is we've been told that people who  have safety deposit boxes will actually  be able to remove all of the contents  all the valuables and cash because there  were questions about that so I imagine  there'll be a lot of people queuing up  is thought to be more than 200,000  safety deposit boxes in banks across the  country people are being asked to make  appointments first to stop sort of a  stampede so we'll see how that goes but  the whole point of this is as we say  capital controls passed in place they'd  like to be in place for quite some time  it's more of a psychological move to  sort of allow Greeks the feel but 